    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `TypeChecker._includes_version_compatible_overload`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_includes_version_compatible_overload` function checks if a set of overloads for an operator includes one that is compatible with the configured Python version. This is important because Astroid, the static analysis library used by pylint, may infer the existence of certain methods like `__or__` or `__ror__` on builtins.type, which are only available in Python 3.10 and later. The function takes a list of `nodes.NodeNG` objects as its parameter, representing the attributes to check. It returns a boolean indicating whether the set of overloads includes a version-compatible overload. The function uses a generator expression within the `all` function to determine if all attributes are either function definitions or bound methods belonging to the builtins.type. It then returns `True` if the attributes are not all built-in methods for Python 3.10 or if the pylint configuration is set for Python 3.10 or later. The function does not handle any errors or edge cases explicitly, and it has no side effects.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
